.Ss expr expressions
Many operators must be quoted or escaped for the shell.
Comparisons are arithmetic when both operands are numbers and
lexicographical otherwise.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar ARG1 Cm \&| Ar ARG2
Return ARG1 if it is neither null nor zero; otherwise return ARG2.
.It Ar ARG1 Cm & Ar ARG2
Return ARG1 if neither operand is null or zero; otherwise return zero.
.It Ar ARG1 Cm < Ar ARG2
Return 1 if ARG1 is less than ARG2, and 0 otherwise.
The other comparisons are
.Ql <= ,
.Ql = ,
.Ql != ,
.Ql >= ,
and
.Ql > .
.It Ar ARG1 Cm + Ar ARG2
Add the operands.
The other arithmetic operators are
.Ql - ,
.Ql * ,
.Ql / ,
and
.Ql % .
.It Ar STRING Cm \&: Ar REGEXP
Match a regular expression at the beginning of STRING.
.Ql match STRING REGEXP
is equivalent.
Return the text matched between
.Ql \e(
and
.Ql \e) ,
or null if it does not match.
Without those delimiters, return the number of characters matched,
or zero.
.It Cm substr Ar STRING POS LEN
Extract a substring; positions start at 1.
.It Cm index Ar STRING CHARS
Return the position of the first character from CHARS in STRING,
or zero.
.It Cm length Ar STRING
Return the string length.
.It Cm quote Ar TOKEN
Treat TOKEN as a string even if it is an operator or keyword.
.It Pq Ar EXPRESSION
Evaluate the enclosed expression.
